{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Adam lived, etc.</b> In the days of their lives Scripture made three divisions, for in every case it mentioned the number of the days of their lives before they begot children, and afterward gathered them together, and in every case it mentioned ״וַיָּמֹת״ ('and he died'). But in the perasha of Noah it mentioned only two divisions and did not mention there 'and he died' — because one who is without children is called dead in his lifetime, and likewise the wicked, who do not beget offspring of good deeds, are called dead in their lifetime. Therefore it used the expression וַיְחִי ('and he lived') for the number of days they lived before they begot children, for even though they were still without children, nevertheless their lives were truly alive, inasmuch as the entire chain that is mentioned in this perasha and in the perasha of Noah were all righteous — Adam, Seth, and so forth, all of them. And after they begot children, vitality was added to them on account of the children; therefore it mentioned 'and he lived' for the days they lived after they begot children, and then it gathered them all together, to say that all of them were equal for good, for before the Flood no deficiency was found among them except death itself, as it is written (Job 21:11) ״יְשַׁלְּחוּ כַצֹּאן עֲוִילֵיהֶם״ ('They send forth their little ones like a flock'), and it is written there (Job 21:13) ״יְבַלּוּ בַטּוֹב יְמֵיהֶם״ ('They spend their days in prosperity'). And furthermore, it mentioned 'and he died' in every case, to indicate that all of them died on their beds and were not swept away in the Flood. But after the Flood the world changed and all their days were not equal for good; therefore it did not gather them together again, nor did it mention 'and he died,' for there were deficiencies in all of them besides death. And furthermore, after the Flood there is no need to mention 'and he died,' for it is self-evident that they died, and what man shall live and not see death? And it seems right in my eyes to say that the entire chain that is mentioned were prophets, for who told Lamech that this son would comfort us from our deeds and from the toil of our hands, and likewise Shem son of Noah, and so too Enoch and Methuselah and Eber — and they serve as a general principle for all of them."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The verse citation for יְבַלּוּ בַטּוֹב יְמֵיהֶם is given as (שָׁם) 'there,' i.e., the same chapter Job 21; the precise verse is Job 21:13."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:13.401770407Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}